@startuml quality-gates
' The four gates a change passes, and what each one can and cannot catch.
' Grounded in .github/workflows/ci.yml, .clang-tidy, tests/, docs/uml/.

title Quality gates: what each one catches, and what it cannot

skinparam shadowing false
skinparam componentStyle rectangle

rectangle "1. Compiler" as C #ddeeff {
  card "-Wall -Wextra -Werror\n-pedantic, gcc and clang" as Cc
}

rectangle "2. Host tests" as T #ddffdd {
  card "267 checks\ncontract + conversion" as Tt
}

rectangle "3. Static analysis" as S #ffeedd {
  card "clang-tidy\nnaming, size, bug classes" as Ss
}

rectangle "4. Cross-compile" as X #eeddff {
  card "Cortex-M4\ncompile AND link" as Xx
}

rectangle "Only the board" as B #ffdddd {
  card "does the number match\nthe real world" as Bb
}

Cc --> Tt
Tt --> Ss
Ss --> Xx
Xx --> Bb

note right of Cc
  Catches: type errors, unused
  results, implicit conversions.
  Misses: anything that compiles
  and is still wrong.
end note

note right of Tt
  Catches: wrong behaviour, a broken
  wire format, arithmetic that drifts.
  Proven by mutation testing.
  Misses: anything main.c wires up
  wrongly, since main.c is not tested.
end note

note right of Ss
  Catches: a name that stops telling
  a story, a function that runs away,
  a pointer that should be const.
  Missed: the two adjacent uint32_t of
  temperature_deci_celsius, where a swap
  compiled fine and returned a plausible
  wrong number. Fixed by giving them
  distinct types, so gate 1 rejects it
  now. The lesson stands: when a gate
  cannot see a risk, move the risk to a
  gate that can.
end note

note right of Xx
  Catches: a missing symbol, an
  overflowing section. These appear
  at LINK time, never before.
  Misses: everything about runtime.
end note

note right of Bb
  The FPU trap passed all four gates:
  the compiler was told an FPU exists,
  the linker saw nothing wrong, and
  host tests pass because a PC has a
  working FPU. Only the board fails,
  and it fails by going quiet.
end note

note as Honest
  **What this is NOT**
  Not a MISRA checker, and the project
  does not claim MISRA compliance.
  MISRA is a paid standard whose real
  checkers are commercial. A partial
  free approximation presented as
  compliance would be an overclaim.
  This is discipline in the same
  spirit, machine-checked.
end note

@enduml
